The AI ecosystem is overwhelmingly Python-oriented:

PyTorch
Transformers
vLLM
sentence-transformers
Hugging Face
scikit-learn
NumPy
ONNX
TensorRT interfaces
LLM providers

So Python should be the AI/ML worker language.

For example:

MOS
 │
 ├── Entity Extraction Worker
 │       └── Python
 │
 ├── Embedding Worker
 │       └── Python
 │
 ├── Reranking Worker
 │       └── Python
 │
 ├── LLM Worker
 │       └── Python
 │
 └── Reflection Worker
         └── Python

But these workers should communicate through the MOS protocol, not directly manipulate arbitrary database tables.

That's an important distinction. Also if any performance-crital code need to run in this context, Mojo should/could be used, if its prove perfromance gains over Python.
------------------------------------------------------------------

TypeScript should be the client ecosystem

I'd strongly recommend official SDKs:

mos-sdk-rust
mos-sdk-python
mos-sdk-typescript

Then an agent written in LangGraph could simply do something conceptually like:

memory.remember(...)
memory.recall(...)
memory.search(...)
memory.learn(...)

while a TypeScript agent could use:

await mos.memory.remember(...)
await mos.memory.recall(...)

and a Rust agent:

mos.memory().remember(...)

All three ultimately speak the same MOS protocol.
